The utility displays the file system usage for each file argument
and  for  each directory in the file hierarchy rooted in each di‐
rectory argument.  If no file is specified, the  block  usage  of
the  hierarchy rooted in the current directory is displayed.  The
options are as follows: Symbolic links on the  command  line  are
followed.   (Symbolic links encountered in the tree traversal are
not followed.)  All symbolic links  are  followed.   No  symbolic
links  are  followed.  Display an entry for each file in the file
hierarchy.  Display the grand total after all the arguments  have
been  processed.  Display an entry files and directories directo‐
ries deep.  If the flag is specified, the number displayed is the
number of gigabyte (1024*1024*1024 bytes) blocks.  If the flag is
specified, the numbers will be displayed in "human‐readable" for‐
mat.  Use unit suffixes: B (Byte), K (Kilobyte), M (Megabyte),  G
(Gigabyte),  T  (Terabyte)  and P (Petabyte).  Output inode usage
instead of blocks.  All "human‐readable" options are ignored.  By
default, displays the number of blocks as returned by the  system
call, i.e. 512‐byte blocks.  If the flag is specified, the number
displayed is the number of kilobyte (1024 bytes) blocks.  Partial
numbers  of blocks are rounded up.  If the flag is specified, the
number displayed is the  number  of  megabyte  (1024*1024  bytes)
blocks.  Ignore files and directories with user flag set.  Gener‐
ate warning messages about directories that cannot be read.  This
is  the  default behaviour.  Display only the grand total for the
specified files.  Filesystem  mount  points  are  not  traversed.
